Subtask 3.4: Analyze Dead Letter Queue Results
Now examine the technical error path in detail, since it exercises the full retry-then-DLQ pipeline.

Confirm the dead-letter file exists:
echo "=== Dead Letter Queue Files ==="
ls -la output/dead-letter/
Expected result: One file matching DLQ-technical-error-test.txt-<timestamp>.txt.

Inspect the dead-letter file contents:
cat output/dead-letter/DLQ-technical-error-test.txt-*.txt
Expected result: A block formatted as:

=== DEAD LETTER QUEUE MESSAGE ===
Timestamp: <yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss>
Original File: technical-error-test.txt
Retry Count: 3
Error Reason: Database connection failed
Original Message: This message contains technical-error keyword
=== END DLQ MESSAGE ===
The Retry Count: 3 value confirms maximumRedeliveries(3) was fully exhausted before Camel routed the exchange to the Dead Letter Channel.

Verify retry backoff behavior from file timestamps:
Since only one dead-letter file is produced per input message (it is written once, after retries are exhausted), retry timing is verified from the log output rather than multiple files. Re-run the application with output captured to a log file to inspect timing:

timeout 30 mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="com.example.camel.ErrorHandlingApplication" > run.log 2>&1
grep -E "Delivery attempt|redelivery" run.log
Expected result: Multiple log lines referencing redelivery attempts for the technical error message, with increasing gaps between them consistent with redeliveryDelay(1000) and backOffMultiplier(2) (approximately 1s, then 2s, then 4s between attempts).

Confirm redelivery attempts in the log:
grep "CamelRedeliveryCounter\|Delivery attempt" run.log | head -10
Expected result: Log entries showing the redelivery counter incrementing from 1 to 3 for technical-error-test.txt, each logged at WARN level (via retryAttemptedLogLevel(WARN)), followed by one ERROR-level line indicating retries are exhausted (via retriesExhaustedLogLevel(ERROR)).

Interpret the results against the lab objectives:
Cross-check the three outcomes observed so far against the intended behavior:

Scenario	Exception Type	Retries	Final Destination
Normal / retry-success message	none / transient	0 or up to 2	output/success/
Business error message	BusinessException	0 (explicitly disabled)	output/business-errors/
Technical error message	TechnicalException	3 (exhausted)	output/dead-letter/
If all three rows match what you observed in Subtasks 3.2 through 3.4, the Dead Letter Channel, retry policy, and exception-type routing are all working as designed.

Troubleshooting guidance if a scenario does not match:
If output/dead-letter/ is empty but output/business-errors/ has an unexpected extra file, check that the filename in input/ actually contains the substring technical-error and not business-error â�� the processor matches on substring, and files created earlier in the lab could collide if reused.
If no output files appear at all after 30 seconds, confirm the file endpoint polled the correct directory by checking run.log for a line beginning Processing file: â�� if absent, verify input/ contains files directly (not in a subdirectory) since the route uses file:input with noop=true.
