Conclusion
In this lab you built a complete Apache Camel error-handling pipeline: custom business and technical exceptions, a processor that deterministically simulates recoverable and non-recoverable failures, and a Dead Letter Channel route that applies distinct retry policies per exception type. You validated the routing logic with a JUnit 5 test suite and then confirmed the same behavior end to end by running the packaged application, inspecting output files, and reading redelivery counters and backoff timing directly from the logs. Through this process you practiced distinguishing recoverable errors (retried with exponential backoff before landing in a DLQ) from non-recoverable ones (routed immediately for manual review), and you gained hands-on experience troubleshooting a Camel route using both automated tests and manual log/file analysis. From here, natural next steps include integrating a real message broker (such as ActiveMQ or Kafka) in place of the file endpoints, adding metrics or alerting on DLQ volume, and exploring Camel's Circuit Breaker EIP for more advanced resilience patterns.
